f C_J, so all first derivatives vanish there; continuity implies they vanish on the boundary as well. Thus e(·,r) is continuous, bounded on C_J and zero off C_J, proving its Lebesgue integrability. Choose a closed box B containing C_J. On B, e and its time derivative are continuous; the time derivative is uniformly continuous on B×J. For h→0 the one-variable fundamental theorem of calculus writes the difference quotient of e as the average of its time derivative between r0 and r0+h. Uniform continuity gives uniform convergence on B, so finite measure of B allows integration of the limit. Hence E'(r0)=∫_B e_t(x,r0)dx. Differentiating the finite sum defining e gives e_t=2u_t·u_tt+2Σ_j u_j·∂_j u_t. For each j, the smooth compactly supported scalar product u_t·u_j has spatial derivative whose integral is zero: integrate first in x_j by the one-dimensional fundamental theorem of calculus, then in the other variables by Fubini, applicable since the derivative is continuous and compactly supported. Thus ∫u_j·∂_j u_t=−∫u_t·∂_j u_j. Summing yields E'(r0)=2∫u_t·(u_tt−Δ_xu). Differentiating |u|²=1 once in time gives u·u_t=0. By H002, pointwise u_tt−Δ_xu=a u for some scalar a; hence u_t·(u_tt−Δ_xu)=0. The integral is zero. Since r0 was arbitrary the conclusion holds for every positive time.\\\",\\\"uses\\\":[\\\"H001\\\",\\\"H002\\\",\\\"H003\\\"],\\\"uses_local\\\":[],\\\"objects\\\":[\\\"r0\\\",\\\"J\\\",\\\"C_J\\\",\\\"B\\\",\\\"u_t\\\",\\\"u_j\\\",\\\"u_{tt}\\\",\\\"u\\\",\\\"e\\\",\\\"E\\\"],\\\"produces\\\":[],\\\"introduces\\\":[],\\\"discharges\\\":[]}],\\\"applications\\\":[]}]}}\"}","status":"completed"}}

